{:edb/db-before-t 2203 :edb/db-after-t 2204 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535516 50 #inst "2026-10-09T01:38:39.749Z" 13194139535516 true] [17592186133513 1100 "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066555/assets/8a460dfb89b0d2e9c1e197aa212f494971df1c36822117fb4b1271f5608d1d1a.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066555/assets/a172f54b0dc879c2363cfd2818fc79614d68662def6dd56b0ac9aec8fdbca1a5.png) Find the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{b}\\}.$" 13194139535516 true] [17592186133513 1100 "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Find the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{b}\\}$." 13194139535516 false] [17592186133513 1145 "The orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{b}\\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{b}a &= \\frac{b \\cdot a}{b \\cdot b}b \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot 2 + 1 \\cdot 9 + 0 \\cdot (-1) + 1 \\cdot (-1))/(2^{2} + 1^{2} + 0^{2} + 1^{2})b \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 + 9 + 0 - 1}{4 + 1 + 0 + 1}b \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{6}b \\\\\n&= 2b \\\\\n&= 2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535516 false] [17592186133513 1145 "The orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{b}\\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{b\\cdot a}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 9+0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{4+9+0-1}{4+1+0+1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{6}b \\\\  & =2b \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535516 true] [17592186133514 1100 "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066555/assets/fb060fbc7ac20ff3b739a7c8958b4977e72abd31679c098973395db99fb6f78e.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066555/assets/7a20f39cb1c7c36a01e9d7d4dcd8cbe619c8cffacb57bc2bf42b8d46ce53bb5b.png) If $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{y} + \\mathbf{z},$ where $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ and $\\mathbf{z} \\perp \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\},$ then find $\\mathbf{z}.$" 13194139535516 true] [17592186133514 1100 "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ If $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{y} + \\mathbf{z}$, where $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ and $\\mathbf{z} \\perp \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$, then find $\\mathbf{z}$." 13194139535516 false] [17592186133514 1145 "A diagram of the situation is provided below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066555/assets/d887852fe51f08cb7296437e69b6a9632721cfd155558e6abf9a99b4f59f7ad6.png)\n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x & =\\frac{a\\cdot x}{a\\cdot a}a \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot (-4)+0\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4+0-2}{1+1+0+1}a \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3}a \\\\  & =2a \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ z & =x-{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ and $\\mathbf{z} \\perp \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\},$ as shown in the picture above." 13194139535516 true] [17592186133514 1145 "A diagram of the situation is provided below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2122/Source/Images/1a8d9c2a40956965eaef1293fc216d15.png)\n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{a}x &= \\frac{a \\cdot x}{a \\cdot a}a \\\\\n&= (1 \\cdot 4 + (-1) \\cdot (-4) + 0 \\cdot 2 + (-1) \\cdot 2)/(1^{2} + (-1)^{2} + 0^{2} + (-1)^{2})a \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 + 4 + 0 - 2}{1 + 1 + 0 + 1}a \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{3}a \\\\\n&= 2a \\\\\n&= 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= proj_{a}x = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nz &= x - proj_{a}x = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ and $\\mathbf{z} \\perp \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$, as shown in the picture above." 13194139535516 false] [17592186133515 1100 "Given the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the solution space of the system.\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066555/assets/37beee87c5b5bd6350bd2c0f6353b61b1cfd321449a96b8b66aac99584d459bb.png)" 13194139535516 true] [17592186133515 1100 "Given the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the solution space of the system.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535516 false] [17592186133515 1145 "First, we need to find the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} - R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get $x_2=4x_3$.\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1+4x_3+4x_3= 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1=-8x_3\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-8x_{3} \\\\ 4x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsequently, $\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{b}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot b}{b \\cdot b}b \\\\\n&= ((-10) \\cdot (-8) + 20 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 1)/((-8)^{2} + 4^{2} + 1^{2})b \\\\\n&= \\frac{80 + 80 + 2}{64 + 16 + 1}b \\\\\n&= \\frac{162}{81}b \\\\\n&= 2b \\\\\n&= 2\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535516 false] [17592186133515 1145 "First, we need to find the basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get $x_2=4x_3.$ \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain  \n\n$$\nx_1+4x_3+4x_3= 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_1=-8x_3.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066555/assets/4995b9959cbb8a412fe0ed6198bfa9a82bcda40cd388c4759589e060ec16de1e.png)\nConsequently, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066555/assets/232c58060c7ba67f66564e8f8667fe67a84f6ef886a1c56e2d042224ace9b4db.png) is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{(-10)\\cdot (-8)+20\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1}{{(-8)}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{80+80+2}{64+16+1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{162}{81}b \\\\  & =2b \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535516 true] [17592186133706 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353152 13194139535516 true] [17592186133706 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353159 13194139535516 true] [17592186133706 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353180 13194139535516 true] [17592186133706 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353201 13194139535516 true] [17592186133706 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353229 13194139535516 true] [17592186133707 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353166 13194139535516 true] [17592186133707 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353187 13194139535516 true] [17592186133707 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353194 13194139535516 true] [17592186133707 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353236 13194139535516 true] [17592186133708 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353173 13194139535516 true] [17592186133708 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353208 13194139535516 true] [17592186133708 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353215 13194139535516 true] [17592186133708 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353222 13194139535516 true] [17592186134158 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535516 true] [17592186134158 110